e flagpole and the woman, along with their shadows, form two sets of similar triangles. This means that the ratios of corresponding sides of these triangles are equal.\n\nLet's denote:\n- The height of the flagpole as \\( F = 20 \\) feet.\n- The height of the woman as \\( W = 6 \\) feet.\n- The length of the woman's shadow as \\( S_w = 9 \\) feet.\n- The length of the flagpole's shadow as \\( S_f \\) feet, which we need to find.\n\nSince the triangles are similar, the ratio of the height of the flagpole to the height of the woman is equal to the ratio of the length of the flagpole's shadow to the length of the woman's shadow. We can write this as:\n\\[\n\\frac{F}{W} = \\frac{S_f}{S_w}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n\\frac{20}{6} = \\frac{S_f}{9}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( S_f \\), we solve the equation by cross-multiplying:\n\\[\n20 \\times 9 = 6 \\times S_f\n\\]\n\\[\n180 = 6S_f\n\\]\n\nNext, we divide both sides by 6:\n\\[\nS_f = \\frac{180}{6} = 30\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the shadow of the flagpole is \\(\\boxed{30}\\) feet."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{30}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:565"}
